<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mathura</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mathura is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, revered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> as the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. In antiquity it was the capital of the Surasena kingdom, then a seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">Satraps</a> and the southern capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>, when its workshops produced some of the earliest Buddha images in red sandstone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> sacked it in 1018, and it remains one of India's main pilgrimage centres.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mathura.jpg" alt="Mathura" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Umang108 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vishram_Ghat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
